Roy G. Krenkel {
	LN=|Roy Gerald Krenkel|
	BD=|1918|
	DD=|1983|
	NT=|In the 1950's, illustrator Krenkel collaborated with friends
	Al Williamson and %%A,Frank Frazetta%% on many comic book stories,
	most notably for EC Comics' science fiction titles.
	He also did illustrations for science fiction magazines.
	In the 60's he did the work for which he is probably best
	known, cover paintings and title page drawings for 20 novels
	by %%A,Edgar Rice Burroughs%% published by Ace Books.
	He also did covers and interior illustrations for a number of
	hardcover editions of Burroughs books published by Canaveral
	Press, and covers for a number of other Ace and Lancer paperbacks.
	In the 70's he illustrated several paperbacks for DAW Books,
	and did lavish illustrations for hardcover editions of
	%%A,Robert E. Howard%%'s historical fiction published by Donald Grant.
	In the 60's and 70's many of his drawings were published
	in "Amra", the Robert E. Howard fanzine, and in various Edgar
	Rice Burroughs fanzines.
	At least 2 books have been devoted to his work, "Cities and
	Scenes from the Ancient World" (1974, Owlswick Press) and
	"Swordsmen and Saurians" (1989, Eclipse Books).|
}
Mildred Clingerman {
	LN=|Clingerman, Mildred McElroy|
	BP=|Allen, Oklahoma, USA|
	BD=|1918|
	DD=|1997|
	NT=|Mildred McElroy Clingerman was
	born in Allen, Oklahoma, on March 14, 1918, and moved with
	her mother and sister to Tucson in 1929. She was a graduate
	of Tucson High School, and attended the University of
	Arizona. She married Stuart Clingerman in 1937. <br>
	Mrs. Clingerman was well-known as a fantasy and science
	fiction writer, and was widely published in Fantasy and
	Science Fiction magazine, as well as Good Housekeeping and
	Colliers. In 1960 she published a paperback edition of her
	short stories entitled <i>A Cupful of Space</i>. Her stories have
	also appeared in several anthologies, including literature
	textbooks for middle and high school students.
	Mrs. Clingerman was a charter member and a founder of the
	Tucson Writer's Club. She was also active in the Tucson
	Press Club, serving several terms on its Board of Directors.|
}
