	But most people think, they are just pests and treat them like this.
	So I'm giving lectures and slide shows since 1994 about
	these stingy insects.
	In 1995 I was honoured with the Sven-Simon-Award for this work.
	But being a Science-Fiction-Fan since Star Trek concoured
	the german TV-Screens, I'm also interested in the concepts of
	alien biology, which isn't so alien at all...
	Who would like to know more about my work and other interests,
	visit my %%H,homepage##http://www.chemie.fu-berlin.de/~annine%%.|
}
Enrique A. Imbert {
	LN=|A. Imbert, Enrique|
	NT=|Enrique Anderson Imbert (or Anderson-Imbert) was a Spanish
	American writer. In Spanish-speaking countries, each
	person has two legal last names, one from the father and
	one from the mother. The paternal last name gives the
	initial letter for alphabetization. In the case of
	this writer, his books in Spanish are catalogued in
	libraries under A. So also should his working name in
	this database, either with or without hyphenation of
	the last names. (David Hildner <dhildner@facstaff.wisc.edu>)<p>
	Note: thanks for the explanation, but there is no way to
	lexically derive this information from the name itself, and
	all alphabetization is done automatically by computer. I'll
	have to special case this particular author to correct the
	problem.|
