Howard V. Brown {
	LN=|Brown, Howard Vachel|
	BP=|USA|
	BD=|1878|
	DD=|1945|
	NT=|Born in Lexington, Kentucky, Brown studied at the Chicago
	Art Institute; his paintings were accepted by the National Academy
	and were featured by the the International Exhibition of American
	Illustrators. Brown was the cover illustrator for
	<i>Scientific American</i> from 1913-1931. He began some illustrations
	for Gernsback's <i>Electrical Experimenter</i> 1916-1917, then
	started illustrating SF for <i>Argosy</i> and <i>Science and Invention</i>
	in 1919. He did every cover for Tremain's <i>Astounding Stories</i>
	from issue January 1934 through May 1937, and did about half of the
	covers through November 1938. After Gernsback lost <i>Wonder Stories</i>
	and his main artist %%A,Frank R. Paul%% stopped doing covers for that
	magazine, Brown did every cover from August 1936 through August 1940,
	with the exception of the August 1937 issue, which was done by
	%%A,H. W. Wesso%%.|
}
