Stanley Meltzoff {
	LN=|Meltzoff, Stanley|
	BP=|New York City, NY, USA|
	BD=|1917|
	NT=|Originally intending to become an art historian,
	Meltzoff attended the City College of New York, New
	York University's Institute of Fine Art, and the Art
	Student's League. In the 1930's he began doing artwork
	for mainstream magazines such as <i>Life</i>,
	<i>The National Geographic</i>, and <i>The Saturday
	Evening Post</i>, and was the main cover illustrator for
	<i>Scientific American</i> during the 1960's. Two of his
	most-important students were %%A,Paul Lehr%% and
	%%A,John Schoenherr%%.
	Although highly influential, Meltzoff claims to have only
	produced about seven genre-related works.|
}
